Translation patch: `MyDeserialize for RowDeserializer<T, Text>` and `RowDeserializer<S, Binary>`

Change:
  - Text: the loop moves into a helper `deserialize_text_values(columns.len(), buf)` that returns the values.
  - Binary: the loop `for (i, column) in columns.iter().enumerate()` becomes a recursive helper `deserialize_bin_values(&columns, &bitmap, buf, 0, Vec::with_capacity(columns.len()))`. It handles column `i` and then calls itself on `i + 1`, with the call at the end of both branches of `if bitmap.is_null(i)`. At `i == columns.len()` it returns the values.
Why:
  - Text: Aeneas fails ("Could not match the contexts") on the loop when its result is used together with `columns` after it (as in patch 11).
  - Binary: inside a loop, Aeneas does not support live shared borrows of `columns` (each `Column` contains an `Arc<ColumnMeta<'static>>`) and of the bitmap (which holds a `Cow<'de, [u8]>`): "Found a case of unsupported nested borrows". A recursion needs no loop fixpoint. Aeneas does not model `enumerate` either (see patches 04/05).
Behaviour: Identical: the same values are deserialized from `buf` in the same order, with the same early `Err` return. The recursion visits `i = 0, 1, .., len - 1` exactly as the loop does.
Stack depth (found in review): `deserialize_bin_values` recurses once per column, so the patched copy uses O(columns) stack where the original loop uses constant stack (differential testing: a debug build on a 2 MB thread overflows at ~8000 columns). Results, effect order and errors are identical; the Lean model does not model the stack, and the specs say nothing about stack exhaustion. This difference exists only in the translated copy, not in the shipped crate.

